export anchor Components:
    description:
        Components are roughly equivalent to classes in CSS.

        The purpose of components is to declare and apply styling, to control
        the availability and look of components.

    keywordIdentifier: component

    keyword:
        Components are declared with the ${this.keywordIdentifier} keyword

    identifier:
        Following the keyword is the name of the component.

    indentation:
        Components syntax are indentation-based.  This style is particularly
        well suited for components, because components are little more than a
        single-level of CSS-like properties.

        Indentation follows the same rule as Piton. Tabs or any number of
        spaces may be used, but the choice must be consistent throughout the
        file. Four spaces per level is preferred, and is what the formatter
        writes.

    declaration:
        A declaration is an optional `export`, the keyword, the name, an
        optional extends clause, and a colon. Every line indented beneath it belongs to the component,
        and the component ends at the first line that is not indented.

    naming:
        Component names are written in PascalCase, such as `MyButton`. A name
        must be unique within the file it is used in, counting both the
        components declared there and those imported, because compositions
        refer to components by name alone. An import can be aliased to avoid
        a clash, as described in @{Modules}. Composition names follow the same
        rules.

    propertySyntax:
        Each indented line sets one property, written as the property's
        identifier, a colon, and a value, such as `paddingX: 12px`.

        Identifiers are the camelCase form of the matching CSS property, so
        CSS `flex-direction` is written `flexDirection`. Values are written as
        they would be in CSS. Each property's own entry below lists the values
        it accepts.

        A property may be set only once in a component. The order of lines
        has no meaning.

    shorthands:
        Some properties are shorthands that set several others at once, such
        as `padding` or `border`. When a component sets both a shorthand and
        one of the properties it covers, the more specific property wins,
        whatever order the lines are in. This differs from CSS, where the
        later line wins.

    defaults:
        A property that is not set takes its initial value, as listed in the
        property's entry. Initial values are as close to unset as possible:
        no margin, no padding, no border, no background, and no browser
        default styling. A component with no properties draws nothing but its
        content. The few properties that need some value to draw with, such
        as `color`, `fontFamily` and `fontSize`, have plain fixed initial
        values.

    noCascading:
        Properties never cascade. A component's properties apply to its own
        instances only, and never pass down to the instances inside them. A
        `color` on a card does not colour the text of the buttons in it.

        Extending is the only way for one component to take properties from
        another. Components that share a look should extend a common
        component, as described below.

    values:
        How values are written, the units and colour formats accepted, and how
        a value is split into parts, are described in @{Values}.

    extends:
        Components use structural inheritance, the same as Piton anchors. A
        component may extend one or more other components, listed after
        `extends` and separated by commas, as in
        `component MyButton extends DefaultButton, Rounded:`.

    inheritance:
        - merging
            The new component starts with every property of the components it
            extends. When more than one of them sets the same property, they
            are merged left to right, and the last in line wins.
        - overriding
            Any property the component sets itself replaces the inherited
            value.
        - shorthands
            After merging, the shorthand rule above still applies. A specific
            property, such as `borderColor`, wins over a shorthand that covers
            it, such as `border`, wherever each one came from.
        - chains
            A component may extend a component that extends others, to any
            depth.
        - cycles
            A component can't extend itself, directly or through other
            components. That is a compiler error.
        - visibility
            Only a component declared in the same file, or imported into it,
            can be extended.

    inheritanceExample:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component Rounded:
            borderRadius: 999px
            padding: 4px 8px

        component Spacious:
            padding: 12px 24px

        component Pill extends Rounded, Spacious:
            backgroundColor: #e4e4e7
        \\\
        ```

        `Pill` has a `borderRadius` of `999px` from `Rounded`. Both bases set
        `padding`, and `Spacious` is last in line, so `Pill` gets
        `12px 24px`.

    noNesting:
        Components hold properties only. They do not contain other components,
        text, or selectors; arranging components inside one another is the
        job of compositions. Pseudo-classes, states such as hover or focus,
        and media queries are out of scope.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component Card:
            display: flex
            flexDirection: column
            gap: 12px
            padding: 24px
            backgroundColor: #ffffff
            border: 1px solid #e4e4e7
            borderRadius: 12px
            boxShadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08)

        component FeaturedCard extends Card:
            borderColor: #3b82f6
            backgroundColor: #eff6ff
        \\\
        ```

        `FeaturedCard` has every property of `Card`, but with a blue border
        and a pale blue background. Its `borderColor` wins over the colour set
        by `Card`'s `border` shorthand, because it is the more specific
        property.

    properties:
        layout:
            display:
                - @{Display}
            flex:
                - @{Flex}
                - @{FlexDirection}
                - @{FlexWrap}
                - @{FlexGrow}
                - @{FlexShrink}
                - @{FlexBasis}
            grid:
                - @{GridTemplateColumns}
                - @{GridTemplateRows}
                - @{GridTemplateAreas}
                - @{GridAutoRows}
                - @{GridColumn}
                - @{GridRow}
                - @{GridArea}
            alignment:
                - @{AlignItems}
                - @{AlignSelf}
                - @{AlignContent}
                - @{JustifyContent}
                - @{JustifyItems}
                - @{JustifySelf}
            gap:
                - @{Gap}
                - @{RowGap}
                - @{ColumnGap}
            position:
                - @{Position}
                - @{Top}
                - @{Right}
                - @{Bottom}
                - @{Left}
                - @{Inset}
                - @{ZIndex}
        box:
            sizing:
                - @{Width}
                - @{Height}
                - @{MinWidth}
                - @{MinHeight}
                - @{MaxWidth}
                - @{MaxHeight}
                - @{AspectRatio}
            spacing:
                - @{Margin}
                - @{MarginTop}
                - @{MarginRight}
                - @{MarginBottom}
                - @{MarginLeft}
                - @{Padding}
                - @{PaddingTop}
                - @{PaddingRight}
                - @{PaddingBottom}
                - @{PaddingLeft}
            border:
                - @{Border}
                - @{BorderWidth}
                - @{BorderStyle}
                - @{BorderColor}
                - @{BorderTop}
                - @{BorderRight}
                - @{BorderBottom}
                - @{BorderLeft}
            borderRadius:
                - @{BorderRadius}
            overflow:
                - @{Overflow}
        typography:
            font:
                - @{FontFamily}
                - @{FontSize}
                - @{FontWeight}
                - @{FontStyle}
            linesAndWrapping:
                - @{LineHeight}
                - @{WhiteSpace}
                - @{TextOverflow}
                - @{LineClamp}
            alignmentAndSpacing:
                - @{TextAlign}
                - @{LetterSpacing}
            decoration:
                - @{TextDecoration}
            textAppearance:
                - @{Color}
                - @{TextTransform}
                - @{TextShadow}
        background:
            - @{BackgroundColor}
            - @{BackgroundImage}
            - @{BackgroundSize}
            - @{BackgroundPosition}
            - @{BackgroundRepeat}
        effects:
            - @{Opacity}
            - @{BoxShadow}
        transforms:
            - @{Translate}
            - @{Rotate}
            - @{Scale}
        media:
            - @{ObjectFit}
            - @{ObjectPosition}
        html:
            - @{HtmlTag}
